# MPHam

MPHam provides spin–orbit-coupled DFT Hamiltonians in an atomic-orbital basis, used to train and test
[UniTB: Physical structure as an organizing principle for universal Hamiltonian learning](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36135).
All structures come from the [Materials Project](https://materialsproject.org/). The entry also holds the UniTB model of
the paper with its source code, a packed Python environment that runs it without compilation, and a reproducible
band-structure example (see "UniTB: model, code, environment and a band-structure example").

| Subset | Structures | Role | Data | Archive parts |
|---|---:|---|---:|---:|
| `MPHam-NextHam-test` | 3,000 | Test set: the full NextHAM test benchmark, with recomputed labels | 16.52 GB | 5 |
| `MPHam-12K` | 11,953 | Training set: the NextHAM training list after deduplication | 62.00 GB | 17 |
| `MPHam-29K` | 29,242 | Training set: MPHam-12K plus 17,289 MP-20-derived structures | 131.75 GB | 36 |
| `MPHam-hard` | 500 | Benchmark: complex crystals newly added to the Materials Project, as DFT outputs (see below) | 195.88 GB | 52 |

Sizes are decimal GB (LMDB data for the first three subsets). MPHam-12K is contained in MPHam-29K; it is offered separately
for convenience (see "Using MPHam-12K from an MPHam-29K download" below). MPHam-hard is licensed differently from the rest
(see "License").

## Labels

The labels were computed with ABACUS using PBE, fully relativistic PseudoDojo ONCV pseudopotentials, spin–orbit coupling,
a 120 Ry cutoff and an SCF density threshold of 1e-6. For the NextHAM-list structures (MPHam-NextHam-test and MPHam-12K),
the k-point setting is as in Appendix H of NextHAM. The 17,289 MP-20-derived structures added in MPHam-29K use Γ-centred grids
with a k-point spacing of 0.08 bohr⁻¹.

Each record stores the real spin-up–spin-up block of H − H(0), H − P, H(0) and P on every atom and on every retained edge,
where P is the McWEDA-corrected non-self-consistent prior. The dimensionless
overlap S is stored in aligned sidecar shards. Energies are in eV; positions and cells are in Å.
Values are float32 with 729 shell-pair-packed slots per atom or edge; decode them with the supplied orbital map
(`basis.json`, `slot_map.json`), not as a row-major 27 × 27 matrix. Records contain only edges within the training cutoff and
cannot reconstruct the complete complex spinor Hamiltonian. `FORMAT.md` gives the full record format.

## Download and unpack

The file list on this page shows only the first 100 of the 116 files of this entry. Every file can be downloaded from
`https://store.aissquare.com/datasets/cb9f845b-3c87-4969-85c7-6f0ba027d9d2/<file name>`, and `MANIFEST.sha256` lists all
of them with their SHA-256. `MPHam.docs.tar` holds the small files that describe and index the data: `FORMAT.md`, the
reader `read_example.py`, the orbital maps, `RECORDS.jsonl`, `SHARD_MAP.json`, the files for reading MPHam-12K from
MPHam-29K and the deduplication records. It unpacks into the current directory. Get these two files first, then the parts
you need:

```bash
base=https://store.aissquare.com/datasets/cb9f845b-3c87-4969-85c7-6f0ba027d9d2
wget "$base/MANIFEST.sha256" "$base/MPHam.docs.tar"
tar -xf MPHam.docs.tar
grep ' MPHam-29K\.part' MANIFEST.sha256 | awk -v b="$base" '{print b "/" $2}' | wget -c -i -   # one subset
# awk -v b="$base" '{print b "/" $2}' MANIFEST.sha256 | wget -c -i -                           # everything, about 411 GB
sha256sum -c MANIFEST.sha256 --ignore-missing      # check what you downloaded
```

Data files are named `<subset>.partNNN.tar`. Every part is a plain tar archive (the records inside are already
zstd-compressed) holding complete shards, main and overlap together, so the parts of a subset can be extracted in any order
into one directory:

```bash
for f in MPHam-29K.part*.tar; do tar -xf "$f"; done
```

This creates `MPHam-29K/train/data.train.sNNN.lmdb/data.mdb` and `MPHam-29K/overlap_sidecar/train/data.train.sNNN.lmdb/data.mdb`;
the other subsets follow the same pattern (`MPHam-NextHam-test/test/...`). `SHARD_MAP.json` gives the part, size and SHA-256 of
every shard, and `RECORDS.jsonl` lists every structure with its shard and shard-local index.

To read two records (requires `numpy`, `lmdb` and `zstandard`):

```bash
python read_example.py MPHam-NextHam-test/test/data.test.s000.lmdb \
  --overlap-shard MPHam-NextHam-test/overlap_sidecar/test/data.test.s000.lmdb --count 2
```

Only unpickle records after checking the files against `MANIFEST.sha256`.

## Using MPHam-12K from an MPHam-29K download

The 11,953 structures of MPHam-12K are 11,953 of the 29,242 training structures of MPHam-29K. With MPHam-29K downloaded,
`MPHam-12K_in_29K.csv` gives each of them its 29K shard and shard-local index, and `use_12k_from_29k.py` reads exactly those records
(both files are in `MPHam.docs.tar`):

```bash
python use_12k_from_29k.py --root MPHam-29K --list MPHam-12K_in_29K.csv --count 2
python use_12k_from_29k.py --root MPHam-29K --list MPHam-12K_in_29K.csv --all --overlap
```

The 12K structures are spread over 190 of the 229 training shards of MPHam-29K. Every numerical array of these records is
byte-identical in both subsets (checked for all 11,953 records); only the shard-local `idx` and the `provenance` field differ.

## Deduplication

The test set keeps all 3,000 NextHAM test entries. Two structures appear twice in it, once under a truncated Materials Project ID;
skipping `mp-16504` and `mp-63` gives the 2,998-entry deduplicated benchmark. The training sets exclude records that duplicate a
test structure or another training structure. `DEDUPLICATION.md`, `TRAINING_EXCLUSIONS.jsonl` and `NEXTHAM_TRUNCATED_IDS.jsonl`
(in `MPHam.docs.tar`) list every case. In the `provenance` field of the training records, notes that pointed to internal working files now point to
`DEDUPLICATION.md`; all numerical arrays are byte-identical to the data used in the paper.

## MPHam-hard

`MPHam-hard` is a benchmark of 500 complex nonmagnetic crystals that the Materials Project added in its database release
v2026.04.13 (30–80 atoms and four or five elements per primitive cell; no identifier or reduced formula shared with
MPHam-29K or MPHam-NextHam-test). Unlike the three LMDB subsets it is distributed as DFT outputs: for every crystal an
ABACUS reference in the basis of the MPHam labels (H, S and the non-self-consistent prior H(0) as sparse matrices) and an
OpenMX reference in the basis of Uni-HamGNN (`openmx.scfout`), both with spin–orbit coupling on the same Γ-centred k mesh,
together with reference band energies, band-edge effective masses and the per-structure errors of the models compared on
MPHam-hard, computed for the UniTB paper with the earlier SE(3) version of UniTB. The per-structure results of the released
E3-only model are in `UniTB.tar` (`UniTB/eval/`).

| Group | Content | Size | Archive parts |
|---|---|---:|---:|
| `MPHam-hard.meta` | `MPHam-hard.csv`, structures, DFT settings with pseudopotentials and orbitals, reference eigenvalues, evaluation tables, readers, `README.md`, `FORMAT.md`, `SHA256SUMS` | 2.04 GB | 1 |
| `MPHam-hard.abacus` | ABACUS inputs and logs, H(R), S(R), H(0)(R) | 90.61 GB | 24 |
| `MPHam-hard.openmx` | OpenMX inputs, outputs and `openmx.scfout` | 103.23 GB | 27 |

```bash
for f in MPHam-hard.meta.part*.tar; do tar -xf "$f"; done      # add the .abacus / .openmx parts as needed
sha256sum -c MPHam-hard/SHA256SUMS --ignore-missing
```

The `meta` part alone is enough to run a model on the structures and compare its band energies with both references;
`MPHam-hard/README.md` explains which files inference, evaluation, recomputation and training need. A tool and a tutorial for
converting ABACUS outputs such as these into LMDB records like those of the other subsets will come with a later release of
the UniTB code.
Results reported on MPHam-hard should come from models that were not trained on it.

## UniTB: model, code, environment and a band-structure example

UniTB is the Hamiltonian model of the paper that introduces MPHam. Three files of this entry hold the released
model, everything needed to run it, and a reproducible band calculation:

| File | Size | Content |
|---|---|---|
| [`UniTB.tar`](https://store.aissquare.com/datasets/cb9f845b-3c87-4969-85c7-6f0ba027d9d2/UniTB.tar) | 171,858,944 bytes | folder `UniTB/`: the two networks of the model with their training configuration, validation records and model card (`checkpoint/`); the source code of UniTB and SO2CUDA (`code/`); per-structure evaluation results (`eval/`); the band example (`example_bands/`); `README.md`, `ENVIRONMENT.md`, `SHA256SUMS` |
| [`UniTB.env.tar.gz.part000`](https://store.aissquare.com/datasets/cb9f845b-3c87-4969-85c7-6f0ba027d9d2/UniTB.env.tar.gz.part000) | 3,900,000,000 bytes | first part of the packed Python environment (Linux x86_64); SHA-256 `60c06ce0de288ce3db3241fa167648e43476913abc182ef0e2eb8477eca12f7d` |
| [`UniTB.env.tar.gz.part001`](https://store.aissquare.com/datasets/cb9f845b-3c87-4969-85c7-6f0ba027d9d2/UniTB.env.tar.gz.part001) | 511,978,153 bytes | second part; SHA-256 `09858714dc553e210cc9b25abaab77f908599cc1d5c0bb67b3d6c818edcb499d` |

**The model.** UniTB predicts the self-consistent correction Re[(H − H(0))↑↑] of the ABACUS
Hamiltonian (real spin-up–spin-up block, eV) from the zeroth-step Hamiltonian H(0) and the
overlap S, in the 729-slot layout of the MPHam records (`FORMAT.md`). The complete spin–orbit
Hamiltonian is H(0) plus this correction in both spin-diagonal blocks. The released model is
the E3-only UniTB: two E(3)-equivariant networks (onsite and hopping blocks, 8,029,935
parameters each) trained on MPHam-29K for 91,800 updates. It replaces the SE(3) model of the
first version of the paper, which is kept only as a comparison.

| Benchmark | Result |
|---|---|
| NextHAM test set, 3,000 structures: onsite / hopping / combined matrix error | 5.866 / 0.4282 / 0.4870 meV |
| band200 (200 of these structures, bands after the b = 10 eV projection): fw10, mean | 47.7 meV |
| MPHam-hard (500 crystals): combined matrix error / fw10, means | 0.808 / 38.9 meV |

Matrix errors are mean absolute errors over the valid entries of each structure, averaged over
structures; fw10 is the mean absolute eigenvalue error within ±10 eV of the DFT valence-band
maximum. Per-structure numbers are in `UniTB/eval/`.

**Run it with the packed environment.** The environment contains Python 3.10, PyTorch 2.8.0
with its CUDA 12.8 libraries, UniTB and SO2CUDA, with their CUDA extensions compiled for sm_70
to sm_120 (the FP64 tensor-core kernels of the GF prior from sm_80); it needs no compiler, CUDA
toolkit or network access. Requirements: Linux x86_64 with glibc 2.28 or newer; for GPUs an
NVIDIA GPU of compute capability 7.0 or newer with a driver for CUDA 12.8 (older GPUs: use the
CPU); 13 GB of free disk space; the path of `unitb-env` may be long (tested: 15 to 250 characters). Download `UniTB.tar` and the two environment parts,
then:

```bash
tar -xf UniTB.tar                                                     # -> UniTB/
cat UniTB.env.tar.gz.part000 UniTB.env.tar.gz.part001 | tar -xzf -   # -> unitb-env/ (7.9 GiB)
source unitb-env/bin/activate
conda-unpack                                                          # once, right after unpacking
cd UniTB/example_bands
python predict_bands.py                                   # CPU, about 25 s
python predict_bands.py --device cuda --require-so2cuda   # GPU
# RbMgF3 (mp-8402): fw10 UniTB = 14.7681 meV (reference 14.7681 meV, tolerance 0.050 meV: OK); H(0) alone = 982.6 meV
# (the fourth decimal varies between CPUs and GPUs, 14.7679 to 14.7682: float32 rounding)
```

The example predicts the Hamiltonian of RbMgF3 (mp-8402), one of the test crystals of the
paper's band figure, solves its bands with spin–orbit coupling on the DFT k path, compares
them with DFT and writes the figure to `output/bands_RbMgF3_mp-8402.png`. The reference value
is the model's own result for this crystal in the band200 evaluation.

**Install from source instead.** `UniTB/code/INSTALL.md`: a Python 3.10 environment, the pinned
packages of `UniTB/code/requirements.txt` (PyTorch 2.8.0, torch-scatter 2.1.2, e3nn 0.5.8, …),
then `pip install --no-deps ./SO2CUDA` and
`POETRY_DYNAMIC_VERSIONING_BYPASS=2.0.1+725fbf9 pip install --no-deps ./UniTB`. Installed this
way, the GPU kernels are compiled at first use and need the CUDA 12.8 toolkit.

**Faster inference.** The recommended execution options compute the same numbers (to float32
rounding) along fused GPU kernels; set them after building a network:

```python
from dptb.nnops.speed import SpeedConfig, configure_model
from dptb.nn.embedding.unitb.recipe import UNITB_RECIPE
configure_model(model, SpeedConfig(**UNITB_RECIPE["train_options"]["speed"]))
```

On a GPU the SO2CUDA kernels are used automatically (`SO2_CUDA_BACKEND=off` selects the
PyTorch reference path). Training configurations with `method: unitb` use these options by
default. `UniTB/checkpoint/MODEL_CARD.md` shows the loading code, `UniTB/ENVIRONMENT.md`
describes the options.

**Licenses and citation.** The weights, the evaluation tables and the example are released
under CC BY 4.0, like MPHam-12K, MPHam-29K and MPHam-NextHam-test. The UniTB and SO2CUDA code
is licensed under LGPL-3.0 (UniTB repository DeePTB-Lab/UniTB, commit `725fbf9`, in a copy that
differs in 26 strings listed in `UniTB/code/INSTALL.md`; SO2CUDA repository DeePTB-Lab/SO2CUDA,
commit `0578dff`); third-party code inside UniTB keeps its own license (`UniTB/code/INSTALL.md`). The environment redistributes third-party packages,
unchanged, under their own licenses, among them PyTorch (BSD-3-Clause), Triton (MIT), the
NVIDIA CUDA runtime libraries as published on PyPI (`nvidia-*-cu12`, NVIDIA licenses), Python
(PSF) and GCC (GPL-3.0 with the runtime library exception); `UniTB/ENVIRONMENT.md` lists every
package with its license. Please cite M. Liu, Z. Zhouyin, G. Wang, D. Zheng, L. Shen and Q. Gu,
*Physical structure as an organizing principle for universal Hamiltonian learning*,
arXiv:2610.36135.

